Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 18:18:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?duck-brain=20P20:=20the=20duck's=20board=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=94=20ToF=20on=20i2c-5,=20sound=20on=20`duck`,=20gentle?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20sound=20ends,=205=20GHz=20kept?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Alex's board of 04-05.11.2026, as defaults rather than flags:
- tofd looks for the ToF on /dev/i2c-5 (I2C5-M0, pins 29/31) only:
  i2c3 carries the body IMU at 200 Hz and the kernel's fusb302.
- [audio] device defaults to `duck` (duck-brain's /etc/asound.conf:
  dmix/dsnoop on card duck-i2s3 at its one format, 48 kHz S32_LE
  stereo) instead of the aic3104 codec this board does not have; the
  `sounds` CLI and the pet-detect default follow. A PCM name is opened
  for capture as it is: `duck,0` is no PCM to ALSA, only `hw:`/`plughw:`
  names get the `,0`.
- robotd feeds every 16-bit wav (bank sounds, robot.say phrases) to
  aplay itself: 50 ms of silence first — the amplifier is enabled ~30 ms
  after the stream starts and would eat the beginning — and 30 ms of
  zeros last; a sound cut short (a new sound, a hush) fades over 20 ms
  and ends in zeros instead of being killed mid-wave. The feeder stays
  at most 100 ms ahead, so a cut still lands within ~0.1 s.
- The tick journal stops at 256 MiB, marking where: it lives in /tmp,
  which is RAM.
- configd keeps a network's band when it replaces its profile, and pins
  a new network to 5 GHz when the scan sees it there: on the duck the
  Wi-Fi and the Bluetooth pad share one radio, and on 2.4 GHz the Wi-Fi
  dropped out whenever the pad was connected.
